    "evidence_text": "This setup\n\nallows for unsupervised open domain question answering.\n\nTo this end, we introduce the negated LAMA dataset.\n\nWe identify and analyze a subset of cloze statements where predictions are different.\n\nWe did the same for a subset of ConceptNet that is easy to negate.\n\nResults\tTable TABREF1 compares the predictions of original LAMA and negated LAMA.\n\nTable TABREF4 gives examples of BERT-large predictions.\n\nand \u201cBirds cannot fly.\u201d.\n\nA key problem is that the LAMA setup does not\n\nallow to refrain from giving an answer.\n\nNo common threshold can be found.\n\nWe give two examples of such patterns.\n\nPeople would refer\n\nIn a case study, they test for correct licensing environments for negative polarity items.\n\nWe take a different approach by focusing on factual knowledge stored in negated statements.\n\ninvestigate the understanding of function words \u2013 among them negation particles \u2013 using an entailment- and classification-based approach.\n\nThe models are fine-tuned to the task of interest.\n\nnegated statements is indirectly acquired during pretraining.\n\nWe try to maximize the change in semantics and invert meaning.\n\nConclusion\tWe show that pretrained language models have problems handling negation.\n\nOutput predictions for the original LAMA query and the negated statement are highly correlated.",
